The fundamental difference comes down to data boundaries and execution timing. Batch processing handles large, bounded chunks of historical data at scheduled intervals,
whereas stream processing handles unbounded, continuous flows of data in real-time exactly as it is generated.

Technical Breakdown :-
Execution Paradigm: Batch pipelines run on a schedule (e.g., an Airflow DAG triggered every night at midnight) to process everything that accumulated over the day.
Stream pipelines run 24/7 as long-running jobs, instantly reacting to events the millisecond they arrive.

Latency vs. Throughput: Batch is optimized for massive throughput over petabytes of data, where a latency of minutes or hours is acceptable.
Stream processing optimizes for ultra-low latency, processing micro-batches or individual events in milliseconds.

Tooling: For batch, we rely on engines like standard Apache Spark, Hive, or Snowflake bulk loads.
For streaming, we use distributed event brokers like Apache Kafka or AWS Kinesis, paired with processing engines like Apache Flink."

Buisness Context :-
The choice is dictated by the business's 'time-to-value' for the data. I use Batch processing for complex, heavy aggregations where immediate answers aren't critical—like generating end-of-month financial reports or retraining a machine learning model on five years of history.
I use Stream processing when the value of the data decays in seconds—like blocking a fraudulent credit card transaction before it clears, or calculating dynamic surge pricing for rideshares.

Note :- 
Historically, companies maintained entirely separate tech stacks for batch and streaming—known as the Lambda architecture—which meant writing and maintaining the same business logic twice.
Today, if I'm building a modern system, I look to unify them. By using an engine like Apache Flink or Spark Structured Streaming, I can write my transformation SQL or code exactly once, and simply point it at a static historical file for batch,
or a live Kafka topic for streaming.
